const llmActionsProps = {"enabled":true,"providers":["claude","chatgpt","perplexity","gemini"],"placement":"header","copyButton":true,"footerLink":true,"markdownBody":"# Stage 08: Map structure {#stage-08-map-structure}\n\nIn this stage the map stops being a flat list. Topics nest under other topics, a group of tasks becomes a sequence with previous and next links, one topic is made a link target only, a reference gets a shorter title in the table of contents, and a relationship table says which concepts, tasks and references belong together. DITA-OT generates the links from all of that, so most of the `<related-links>` you wrote by hand in stage 05 come out of the topics.\n\nEvery change is an edit. The map diff is the lesson; the six topic diffs are the same deletion six times.\n\n**Time:** about 25 minutes. **You need:** stage 07 complete.\n\n## Step 1: Structure the map {#step-1-structure-the-map}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Edit `audacity-guide.ditamap`**\n\n   ```diff {title=\"audacity-guide.ditamap\"}\n   --- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n   +++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n   @@ -8,20 +8,57 @@\n      </topicmeta>\n\n      <topichead navtitle=\"Getting started\">\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n   +    <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\" collection-type=\"family\">\n   +      <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n   +    </topicref>\n        <topicref href=\"topics/installing-audacity.dita\"/>\n      </topichead>\n\n      <topichead navtitle=\"Recording and editing\">\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/removing-background-noise.dita\"/>\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n   +    <topicgroup collection-type=\"sequence\">\n   +      <topicref href=\"topics/preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n   +      <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n   +      <topicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n   +      <topicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n   +    </topicgroup>\n   +    <topicref href=\"topics/removing-background-noise.dita\" linking=\"targetonly\"/>\n      </topichead>\n\n   -  <topichead navtitle=\"Reference\">\n   -    <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n   +  <topichead navtitle=\"Reference\" id=\"reference\">\n   +    <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\" locktitle=\"yes\">\n   +      <topicmeta>\n   +        <navtitle>Audio formats</navtitle>\n   +      </topicmeta>\n   +    </topicref>\n      </topichead>\n   +  <reltable>\n   +    <title>Concept, task and reference links</title>\n   +    <relheader>\n   +      <relcolspec type=\"concept\"/>\n   +      <relcolspec type=\"task\"/>\n   +      <relcolspec type=\"reference\"/>\n   +    </relheader>\n   +    <relrow>\n   +      <relcell>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n   +      </relcell>\n   +      <relcell>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n   +      </relcell>\n   +      <relcell>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n   +      </relcell>\n   +    </relrow>\n   +    <relrow>\n   +      <relcell/>\n   +      <relcell>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n   +      </relcell>\n   +      <relcell>\n   +        <topicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n   +      </relcell>\n   +    </relrow>\n   +  </reltable>\n    </map>\n   ```\n2. **Read the hierarchy**\n\n   - A `<topicref>` inside a `<topicref>` is a child topic. The output nests it in the table of contents and generates a \"Parent topic\" link on the child. *What is digital audio?* is now a child of *What is Audacity?*.\n   - `@collection-type` on the parent says how its children relate. `family` means the children link to each other and to the parent; `sequence` means they are read in order, and each child gets \"Previous topic\" and \"Next topic\" links.\n   - `<topicgroup>` groups `<topicref>`s without adding a heading, which is how the four recording tasks become a sequence without a fifth entry in the table of contents. Compare `<topichead>`, which is a heading.\n   - `@linking=\"targetonly\"` on *Removing background noise* means other topics may link to it, but it generates no links of its own. The other values are `sourceonly`, `normal` (the default) and `none`.\n   - `@id=\"reference\"` on the `<topichead>` gives the heading an id, so it can be referenced like any element with an id; the map is XML too.\n   - `@locktitle=\"yes\"` with a `<navtitle>` inside `<topicmeta>` makes the table of contents show *Audio formats* while the page keeps its own title, *Supported audio formats*. Without `@locktitle`, the navigation title is ignored and the topic's title is used.\n3. **Read the relationship table**\n\n   - `<reltable>` is a table of relationships. Each `<relcolspec>` in the `<relheader>` names a column, here typed `concept`, `task` and `reference`, so the generated links are grouped as \"Related concepts\", \"Related tasks\" and \"Related reference\".\n   - Each `<relrow>` says that everything in its cells is related. In the first row the concept links to both tasks and the reference, and each of them links back. An empty `<relcell/>` in the second row means no concept is involved.\n   - Links are generated in both directions from one statement, and a topic can be related to different neighbours in a different map, which is what a hand-written `<link>` cannot do.\n:::\n\n## Step 2: Remove the links the map now generates {#step-2-remove-the-links-the-map-now-generates}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Edit the six topics** Each deletion is a link that a parent-child relationship, the sequence or the reltable now produces. Only the links with no map equivalent stay: the external ones, the `<linklist>` with its own title in *What is digital audio?*, and two links with a `<desc>` or to a topic outside the reltable.\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/what-is-audacity.dita\n   +++ b/topics/what-is-audacity.dita\n   @@ -23,8 +23,6 @@\n        </section>\n      </conbody>\n      <related-links>\n   -    <link href=\"what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n   -    <link href=\"installing-audacity.dita\"/>\n        <link href=\"https://www.audacityteam.org/\" scope=\"external\" format=\"html\">\n          <linktext>The Audacity website</linktext>\n          <desc>Downloads, the manual and the user forum.</desc>\n   ```\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n   +++ b/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n   @@ -113,7 +113,6 @@\n          <link href=\"preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n        </linklist>\n        <linkpool>\n   -      <link href=\"supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n          <link href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sampling_(signal_processing)\" scope=\"external\" format=\"html\">\n            <linktext>Sampling (signal processing)</linktext>\n          </link>\n   ```\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n   +++ b/topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\n   @@ -68,7 +68,6 @@\n        </postreq>\n      </taskbody>\n      <related-links>\n   -    <link href=\"preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n        <link href=\"removing-background-noise.dita\"/>\n      </related-links>\n    </task>\n   ```\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/trimming-audio.dita\n   +++ b/topics/trimming-audio.dita\n   @@ -45,8 +45,4 @@\n          <p>The unwanted audio is gone and the track plays through without a gap.</p>\n        </result>\n      </taskbody>\n   -  <related-links>\n   -    <link href=\"recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n   -    <link href=\"exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n   -  </related-links>\n    </task>\n   ```\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/exporting-audio.dita\n   +++ b/topics/exporting-audio.dita\n   @@ -57,7 +57,6 @@\n        </example>\n      </taskbody>\n      <related-links>\n   -    <link href=\"supported-audio-formats.dita\"/>\n        <link href=\"trimming-audio.dita\">\n          <desc>Clean up the recording before you export it.</desc>\n        </link>\n   ```\n\n   ```diff {title=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\"}\n   --- a/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n   +++ b/topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\n   @@ -71,7 +71,6 @@\n        </table>\n      </refbody>\n      <related-links>\n   -    <link href=\"exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n        <link href=\"https://manual.audacityteam.org/man/file_formats.html\" scope=\"external\" format=\"html\">\n          <linktext>File formats in the Audacity Manual</linktext>\n        </link>\n   ```\n2. **Read why** A hand-written `<link>` states a relationship inside one of the two topics, once, in one direction. The map states it once for both directions, keeps the topic free of assumptions about its neighbours, and lets the same topic sit in another guide with other neighbours. `<related-links>` stays for what the map cannot say: links to the web, a titled `<linklist>`, and a `<desc>`.\n:::\n\n## Step 3: Update the README, run the gate and look at the output {#step-3-update-the-readme-run-the-gate-and-look-at-the-output}\n\n:::steps\n1. **Change the \"You are on\" line**\n\n   ```diff {title=\"README.md\"}\n   --- a/README.md\n   +++ b/README.md\n   @@ -5,7 +5,7 @@ branch is the previous branch plus one lesson, from a single concept topic to a\n    complete, publish-ready **Audacity User Guide**. The diff between two branches\n    is the lesson.\n\n   -You are on **stage 07 — first map**: a map, a deliverable in `project.json`, and the first DITA-OT build.\n   +You are on **stage 08 — map structure**: hierarchy, sequences, link control and a relationship table.\n\n    ## Stages\n\n   ```\n2. **Format and check**\n\n   ```bash\n   dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita\n   scripts/check-stage.sh\n   ```\n\n   ```\n   == validate-project  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n   10 file(s): 10 valid, 0 invalid.\n\n   == project-health  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n   Root map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\n   Project is healthy: valid, no broken references, keys, orphans, or broken element ids.\n\n   == build  project.json -> /tmp/check-stage-357688 ==\n   all deliverables built\n\n   STAGE OK\n   ```\n3. **Read the generated links** Build with `dita --project=project.json` and open the pages under `out/full/topics/`. The link blocks at the foot of each page are now generated. The text of three of them, as DITA-OT 4.3.5 writes it:\n\n   | Page | Generated links |\n   | --- | --- |\n   | *Recording your first track* | Previous topic: Preparing to record. Next topic: Trimming audio. Related concepts: What is digital audio? Related tasks: Removing background noise. Related reference: Supported audio formats |\n   | *Supported audio formats* | Related concepts: What is digital audio? Related tasks: Preparing to record, Recording your first track, Exporting audio, Trimming audio. Related information: File formats in the Audacity Manual |\n   | *Removing background noise* | none |\n\n   The sequence gives the previous and next links, the reltable gives the typed groups, the hand-written external link survives as \"Related information\", and `@linking=\"targetonly\"` leaves the noise task with no links at all while the recording task still links to it.\n:::\n\n`project-health` checks the reltable like any other reference. Misspell `topics/exporting-audio.dita` in the second `<relrow>` as `topics/export-audio.dita` and run the gate:\n\n```\n== project-health  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\nRoot map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nBroken references (1):\n  /home/you/audacity-guide/audacity-guide.ditamap:56  @href=\"topics/export-audio.dita\"\n\nSummary\n  Broken references             1\nFAIL: project-health found issues\n```\n\n## What you learned {#what-you-learned}\n\n- Nested `<topicref>`s make a hierarchy; `@collection-type=\"family\"` and `\"sequence\"` say how children relate, and the output links accordingly.\n- `<topicgroup>` groups without a heading; `<topichead>` is a heading.\n- `@linking` controls which links a topic gives and receives; `@locktitle=\"yes\"` with `<navtitle>` changes the table of contents but not the page.\n- `<reltable>`, `<relheader>`, `<relcolspec type>`, `<relrow>`, `<relcell>` state relationships once for both directions, typed by information type.\n- Hand-written `<related-links>` are for what the map cannot express.\n\n## Where to go next {#where-to-go-next}\n\n:::cards\n- **[Stage 09: Keys](/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/part-2-maps/stage-09-keys){icon=\"arrow-right\"}**\n  Product name, version and download URL defined once, and links that go through a key instead of a path.\n\n- **[Compare 07 to 08 on GitHub](https://github.com/dogsbay/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial/compare/tutorial/07-first-map...tutorial/08-map-structure){icon=\"github\"}**\n  Exactly what this stage added.\n:::\n","mdUrl":"https://dogsbay.ai/dogsbay-xml-dita-tutorial-docs/part-2-maps/stage-08-map-structure.md"} ?? undefined;
